<p>
Today I learned a <a href="https://emacs.dyerdwelling.family/emacs/20261025153614-emacs--stop-going-to-the-top-isearch-allow-motion/">new Emacs trick from Emacs Dwelling</a>. Well, new to me. It turns out it&rsquo;s been in Emacs since 28.1 so for about four and a half years.
</p>
<p>
Here&rsquo;s the problem. When you call isearch it starts searching at the current point location to the bottom of the file. You <i>can</i> cause it to wrap around and continue searching at the top of the file but this isn&rsquo;t always convenient. As a result, many people always start their search by moving to the top of the buffer first. But this isn&rsquo;t always convenient either. For example, if you quit the search you go back to the top of the file, not where you started.
</p>
<p>
Sometimes you want a bit more control: perhaps you want to start at the first match or the last match and if you quit, you&rsquo;d like to be back where you started. Well this is Emacs so <i>of course</i> you can do that. The nice thing is that it&rsquo;s already built in. There are no packages to install, you just enable it my setting <code>isearch-allow-motion</code> to <code>t</code>.
</p>
<p>
You can start the search at any of:
</p>
<ol class="org-ol">
<li>The current point position</li>
<li>The first match in the file</li>
<li>The last match in the file</li>
<li>The first match before the window</li>
<li>The first match after the window</li>
</ol>
<p>
Take a look at the Emacs Dwelling post to see the details and how to invoke the extra behavior. I don&rsquo;t know why this hasn&rsquo;t become the default. If you don&rsquo;t use the extra capabilities, everything works exactly as before.</p>
